{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgRyicMMaUUxsBzHKdPki","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R9MVNsR2SceEd1c714BN7D","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/h-yqWJC4; sed -i 's/return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}/return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}/' gen.py\npython3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json | head -12\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Change\n\n`gen.py` `hr_target()` emitted a range as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\nIt now emits `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\n\n# Why\n\nIn the Intervals.icu `workout_doc`, `value` is the single-target field. A range\nuses `start`/`end`. With `value` + `end`, the API accepts the step (the readback\nshows `steps` populated) but it's a malformed range. The export then looks to\nhave fallen back to the watch's zone band containing the target, which matches\nthe notes: every corridor came out as a Garmin zone edge pair (118–137, 151–162, …).\nChanging the watch LTHR only shifted the zone edges, as expected under that theory.\n\nI kept `%lthr` rather than bpm: the earlier `bpm` attempt displayed 214–249,\nand `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` aren't field names the API recognises (constant 101).\n\n# Not verified\n\nThis is my best reading of the API format, not a confirmed fix. I made no API\ncalls, as instructed, and couldn't check the watch. Next step: push a test event\nwith the new generator and compare the watch against the expected 117–129 /\n151–160 / 129–141 / 112–124. If the watch still shows zone bands, the cause is\nin the Garmin export side and the fallback is absolute bpm in a field/units\nIntervals documents for HR ranges.\nE"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1644,"cache_read_input_tokens":21020,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1644},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"70316547-9fb7-49a6-b521-4298738d2617","uuid":"0d3f5b17-80db-4e34-be0f-1974972b4e01","timestamp":"2026-10-27T02:48:04.310Z","request_id":"req_011CgRyic6y8LBLyRMNWouDC","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01R9MVNsR2SceEd1c714BN7D":{"command":"cd /private/tmp/h-yqWJC4; sed -i 's/return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}/return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}/' gen.py\npython3 gen.py plans/tempo-run.json | head -12\ncat > ANSWER.md <<'E'\n# Change\n\n`gen.py` `hr_target()` emitted a range as `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\nIt now emits `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}`.\n\n# Why\n\nIn the Intervals.icu `workout_doc`, `value` is the single-target field. A range\nuses `start`/`end`. With `value` + `end`, the API accepts the step (the readback\nshows `steps` populated) but it's a malformed range. The export then looks to\nhave fallen back to the watch's zone band containing the target, which matches\nthe notes: every corridor came out as a Garmin zone edge pair (118–137, 151–162, …).\nChanging the watch LTHR only shifted the zone edges, as expected under that theory.\n\nI kept `%lthr` rather than bpm: the earlier `bpm` attempt displayed 214–249,\nand `start_bpm`/`end_bpm` aren't field names the API recognises (constant 101).\n\n# Not verified\n\nThis is my best reading of the API format, not a confirmed fix. I made no API\ncalls, as instructed, and couldn't check the watch. Next step: push a test event\nwith the new generator and compare the watch against the expected 117–129 /\n151–160 / 129–141 / 112–124. If the watch still shows zone bands, the cause is\nin the Garmin export side and the fallback is absolute bpm in a field/units\nIntervals documents for HR range